WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
CORS is a browser-enforced cross-origin access control mechanism.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:11.560
It lets a server tell a browser whether JavaScript from a particular origin may read certain cross-origin responses.

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.180
That scope is important.

4
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.200
CORS affects browser behavior.

5
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:21.460
It is not a general network security boundary and it does not control every type of client in the same way.

6
00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.140
CORS is not authentication.

7
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:30.880
Allowing an origin does not identify the user, service, device, or application instance.

8
00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:35.940
It only means browser JavaScript from that origin may be allowed to read a response under the policy.

9
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:41.160
The application still needs normal authentication mechanisms to establish who or what is calling.

10
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.520
CORS is not authorization.

11
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:55.380
A policy that allows a frontend origin does not decide which records a user may view, which tenant owns a resource, which operation is permitted, or whether an administrative action is allowed.

12
00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:00.680
Authorization must happen separately on the server, close to the protected action or resource.

13
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.880
CORS is not a firewall and does not make an API private.

14
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:15.700
Non-browser clients, server-side applications, command-line tools, scheduled services, and integration systems are not governed by browser CORS enforcement in the same way.

15
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:27.400
If an API should be private, that privacy needs to come from authentication, authorization, network controls where appropriate, and operational governance rather than CORS alone.

16
00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.720
CORS also does not replace CSRF protection.

17
00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:36.980
Browser requests that include existing credentials and change server-side state need their own review.

18
00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:43.080
CORS controls whether browser code can read certain cross-origin responses.

19
00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:49.180
It does not, by itself, settle the safety of every credentialed browser request.

20
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:52.820
The key engineering message is to keep responsibilities separate.

21
00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.480
Authenticate users and services separately.

22
00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:58.300
Authorize actions and resources separately.

23
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.480
Configure CORS only for browser cross-origin access needs.

24
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:09.700
When those responsibilities are blended together, teams tend to overtrust an origin and under-protect the actual operation.
